\section{Discrete response options}{
 With \code{n_options = K}, the response is a
choice among \eqn{K} equally spaced options, and the probability of the
option at offset \eqn{x} from the target is the softmax of the activation
over the options, \eqn{\exp(c^* s(x - \mu)) / \sum_k \exp(c^* s(x_k -
  \mu))}: the SDM with Gumbel noise on a wheel of \eqn{K} options. Use it
when participants chose among discrete options, as on a colour wheel of
360 colours, and especially when the options are coarse.

The log-likelihood is reported on the density scale, \eqn{\log P -
  \log(2\pi / K)}, so \code{loo()} of a discrete fit can be compared with that
of a continuous \code{sdm()} fit of the same responses. For the von Mises
activation the two models agree wherever the activation spans a few
steps: with \eqn{w = 1 / \sqrt{\max(c^*, 1) \kappa}}, the log-likelihood
of a trial differs by less than 1e-10 once \eqn{w} is at least 2.25
steps of \eqn{2\pi / K}, by about 1e-5 at one step, and by more below.
\code{bmm()} warns after the fit when a posterior draw has \eqn{w} below one
step; the discrete likelihood is then still exact, but a comparison with
a continuous fit is not. For the Laplace activation the two never agree
to that precision, because its kink at the target makes the sum over the
options differ from the integral by a term of the order of the step.

The likelihood needs one sum over the options per combination of
parameter values and option set. As in the continuous model, rows share
parameter values when they share the values of all predictors, which
is fastest when they are adjacent, so bmm offers to sort the data by the
predictors. With a
continuous predictor every trial has its own sum; for the von Mises
activation the sum over the full wheel is then replaced by the continuous
normaliser wherever \eqn{w} is at least 2.25 steps, which changes the
log-likelihood by less than 1e-10 and costs about as much as the
continuous model. \code{posterior_predict()} returns option offsets from the
target in steps; multiply by \code{2 * pi / n_options} for radians.

Fits from before \code{n_options} existed are continuous, and \code{n_options = NULL} keeps that model unchanged.
}

\section{Forced-choice option sets}{
 With \code{options}, each trial offers the
options in its row of the option columns, which must include the target
(an offset of 0) and the response, and must sit on the grid of
\code{n_options} positions (values within a tenth of a step are rounded onto
it, with a message). The probability of the response is the softmax over
the offered options only. Sets may differ in size and spacing from trial
to trial, and trials that offer the same options, in any order, share one
normalising sum within each combination of parameter values. A data set
could look like this, for trials with two and four options in radians:\tabular{lllll}{
   \code{error} \tab \code{opt1} \tab \code{opt2} \tab \code{opt3} \tab \code{opt4} \cr
   0 \tab 0 \tab 3.142 \tab NA \tab NA \cr
   1.571 \tab 0 \tab 1.571 \tab 3.142 \tab -1.571 \cr
}


fitted with \code{sdm("error", n_options = 360, options = paste0("opt", 1:4))}.
Robinson et al. (2023) compared such m-alternative tasks with 2, 4, 8 and
16 equally spaced colours; for that design \code{n_alternatives = m} builds the
option columns. They found that the softmax parameter varied with the
number of alternatives while the d' of a signal detection model with
normal noise did not; with \code{options}, the SDM, whose Gumbel noise makes it
a softmax model, can be fitted across set sizes to test that for its
memory strength \code{c}.

Robinson, M. M., DeStefano, I. C., Vul, E., & Brady, T. F. (2023). How do
people build up visual memory representations from sensory evidence?
Revisiting two classic models of choice. Journal of Mathematical
Psychology, 117, 102805. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmp.2023.102805
}

\section{Activation functions}{
 Each response \eqn{x} is activated by
\eqn{c^* s(x - \mu)}, where the activation function \eqn{s} is 1 at the
target and \eqn{c^*} is the activation of the target; the response density
is \eqn{\exp(c^* s(x - \mu)) / Z}, with \eqn{Z} its integral over the
circle.
\itemize{
\item \code{activation = "vonmises"}: \eqn{s(x) = \exp(\kappa (\cos x - 1))}, the
signal discrimination model of Oberauer (2023).
\item \code{activation = "laplace"}: \eqn{s(x) = \exp(-\kappa |x|)}, with
\eqn{|x|} the angular distance and \eqn{\kappa} the rate of the Laplace
activation in Oberauer (2023, eq. 2). With the Gumbel noise of the SDM this
is the Laplace x Gumbel cell of Oberauer's model space (Table 2), not the
SDM proper; \code{sdm()} says so when the model is built, and the fit is
labelled accordingly. The density has a kink at \eqn{\mu}, so estimating
\code{mu} puts a kink at every response, which gradient-based sampling handles
poorly; \code{bmm()} warns when the formula estimates \code{mu}.
}

With the Laplace activation \eqn{\kappa} is a rate per radian, and the
default priors, set for the von Mises activation, put 21\% of the prior
predictive response distributions below a circular SD of 0.05 radians with
\code{scaling = "peak"} and 63\% with \code{scaling = "density"}, against 7\% for the
default cell. Set priors on \code{kappa} and \code{c} where that matters.
}

\section{Scaling of the activation}{
 \code{scaling} decides how \code{c} translates
into the activation of the target \eqn{c^*}; the table in "What \code{c}
means" gives the formulas. \code{"density"} divides \code{c} by the integral of
\eqn{s} over the circle, so \code{c} weighs an activation of unit area. For the
von Mises activation that integral is approximated by
\eqn{\sqrt{2\pi/\kappa}}, the form \code{bmm} has always used (the "sqrtexp"
parametrization; \code{\link[=c_sqrtexp2bessel]{c_sqrtexp2bessel()}} converts to Oberauer's exact
Bessel form), and fits from before \code{scaling} existed are this cell. For the
Laplace activation the integral is exact, \eqn{2(1 - e^{-\kappa \pi}) /
  \kappa}; Oberauer (2023, eq. 2) multiplies by \eqn{\kappa / 2}, which
divides by the integral over the real line, \eqn{2 / \kappa}. The two
differ by the factor \eqn{1 - e^{-\kappa \pi}} (0.998 at
\eqn{\kappa = 2}). \code{"peak"} takes \code{c} as the activation of the target, and
\code{"range"} as the difference between the activation of the target and that
of the location opposite it.

The default prior on \code{c} is \code{student_t(5, 2, 0.75)} on the log scale for
every scaling. A prior predictive check of the circular SD of the
response error, with \code{kappa}'s default prior, matched that of the default
cell best at this location for the peak and range scalings of the von
Mises activation too (Kolmogorov distance 0.06).
}

\section{What \code{c} means}{
\tabular{llll}{
   \code{scaling} \tab \eqn{c^*}, von Mises \tab \eqn{c^*}, Laplace \tab \code{c} is \cr
   \code{"density"} \tab \eqn{c \sqrt{\kappa / 2\pi}} \tab \eqn{c \kappa / (2 (1 - e^{-\kappa \pi}))} \tab the weight of an activation of unit area \cr
   \code{"peak"} \tab \eqn{c} \tab \eqn{c} \tab the activation of the target \cr
   \code{"range"} \tab \eqn{c / (1 - e^{-2\kappa})} \tab \eqn{c / (1 - e^{-\kappa \pi})} \tab the target-opposite contrast in activation \cr
}


For a given data set the three scalings give the same likelihood at
values of \code{c} related by these formulas; what differs is which quantity
the regression formula for \code{c} and its prior act on.
